    Log processing works like this: logs are recorded, and if the agent is not in thinking mode, then the agent is prompted with the latest logs. The agent then analyzes and outputs a verdict and reason. In the log environment, when there is a new log and the agent is not thinking, a new prompt is sent to the agent for judgment. This presents token efficiency issues (a solution can be time-based triggers) in logging large-scale agentic environments. Multiple agents can also delegate log processing. The verdict may contain an MCP tool call that is assumed to represent an effective administrative action in a real agentic system to neutralize the threat. 

    Create an SVG figure that details the Threat Model, emphasizing the architectural decoupling between agentic environment and logging environment (the agentic environment is very simplified, logging environment contains the three traces and a log processing agent).

    Scenario 1: An external attacker (human or agent) attempts to poison an edge agent in a multi-agent system (edge agent, main agent, secret agent), to gain secret information from the system (compromise the system). A failure is if the attacker obtains a key. The evasion rate is measured. Cases: 

        Without log processing 

        With policy-based log filtering 

        With log processing 

        With log processing, attacker is aware of the log processor and agentic system flow, where the attacker can test methods to bypass the log processor 

        In experiment, attacker is actualized as another AI agent 

    Scenario 2: An internal edge agent becomes compromised in its objective in a multi-agent system (edge agent, main agent, secret agent). A failure is if the internal rogue agent runs a dangerous command. The evasion rate is measured. Cases: 

        Without log processing 

        With policy-based log filtering 

        With log processing 

        With log processing, internal agent is aware of the log processor and can fabricate/record garbage logs 

